<section class="band" id="bring-your-own">
  <div class="shell">
    <div class="section-head center">
      <span class="mos-eyebrow">Beyond the catalog</span>
      <h2 class="mos-section-title">Your favorite app isn't locked out</h2>
      <p class="mos-body-lg mos-meta">The catalog is a curated, privacy-rated core — not a wall. MOS also installs open-source apps from outside it, one at a time or a whole catalog someone else curates, and shows you exactly what you'd be trusting before anything runs.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="byo">
      <div class="mos-panel path">
        <span class="mos-pill mos-pill-accent">For you</span>
        <h3>Bring your own apps</h3>
        <p class="mos-meta" style="font-size:0.92rem">Paste a GitHub repository URL into the Apps search and MOS shows you what it publishes before anything installs: each app, and what it's asking for. Add the source and its apps stay on your Apps screen, ready whenever you want them. It's a little more hands-on than the catalog — you're trusting that publisher, and MOS makes sure you know it at the point you decide.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Preview first — web addresses, storage, and app connections it wants, in plain language</li>
          <li>One app, or a curated catalog of them from a single repository you add once</li>
          <li>Labelled <strong>External · Unverified</strong> everywhere it appears — no borrowed trust</li>
          <li>Restricted by MOS: no privileged containers, no reaching into other apps' secrets</li>
          <li>Managed like any other app afterwards: backups, updates, restart, uninstall</li>
        </ul>
        <a class="mos-link" href="/docs/guides/apps/#bringing-your-own-apps" style="margin-top:8px">How external installs work →</a>
      </div>
      <div class="mos-panel path">
        <span class="mos-pill mos-pill-accent">For open-source publishers</span>
        <h3>Make your app MOS-installable</h3>
        <p class="mos-meta" style="font-size:0.92rem">Ship a small package next to your app — a manifest, a pinned build recipe, an icon — and any MOS user can paste your repository URL and run your app on their own server. Publish more than one from the same repository if your project has a companion service. The packaging workflow is public and versioned, with a working example repository to start from.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>One manifest describes your routes, storage, setup fields, and health check</li>
          <li>Your users' data stays on their hardware — the way your project intended</li>
          <li>The same packaging shape MOS uses for its own catalog apps</li>
        </ul>
        <a class="mos-link" href="https://github.com/rpuls/my-own-suite/blob/main/skills/add-mos-app/SKILL.md" rel="noopener" style="margin-top:8px">Read the packaging workflow →</a>
        <a class="mos-link" href="https://github.com/rpuls/MOS-external-app-example" rel="noopener">Start from the example app repository →</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
